in @prism
in @meta
in @glass
in @io

# @io/fs — the POSIX filesystem @io species (mirror-altitude lift-
# tick of the forward-promise at io.mirror 189-191).
#
# Sibling to @io/cargo (2026-06-05), @io/stagefreight (2026-06-23),
# @io/oci (2026-06-24), @io/git (2026-06-24), @io/algebra
# (2026-06-30), @io/crypto (2026-07-15; landed alongside this
# tick per Seam §D9 REED-INLINE cascade). Same @io species shape
# at the POSIX filesystem altitude: typed contract the mirror side
# sees; opaque non-mirror body (kernel syscalls: open, read,
# write, stat, readdir, mkdir, unlink) discharging at the
# realisation boundary.
#
# [Note: five action names dropped `fs_` prefix per Seam extended-
# scope etymology audit at 5dcad39 §2.5.g-k Q_D — POSIX-inertia:
# no vendor sibling (POSIX is standard-of-one for filesystem), so
# the `fs_` prefix inside `@io/fs` was bureaucratic `fs.fs`
# doubling. Boot floor `boot/std/io.mirror` uses bare `open/read/
# write/close`; store.mirror uses bare `read/write`; cargo.mirror
# uses bare `build/test/check/clippy/audit`. Bilateral `writable`
# renamed → `path_writable` for shard-internal consistency with
# `path_admissible`, `path_exists`. See AGENTS.md §7.6 vendor-
# anchoring meta-rule.]
#
# === Landing metadata ===
#
# Landed 2026-07-15 by Mara as Landing 7 of the @secrets shape-
# proposal sequence (companion audit: `docs/audits/2026-07-15-
# seam-secrets-shape-proposal-phase-d-cascade.md` at 019ad8f,
# ratifying Candidate 2 as Mara-mintable per @io/{cargo, git,
# oci, stagefreight, algebra} precedent chain + @io/git lift-tick
# precedent 2026-06-24 grounding Scenario A). Companion shape-
# proposal at `docs/scouts/2026-07-15-mara-secrets-shape-
# proposal.md` §5.5 step 7.
#
# === The lift-tick recognition ===
#
# `shards/io.mirror` 189-191 verbatim forward-promised this shard:
#
#   "@io/fs         POSIX filesystem surface (open / read / write /
#                    stat / readdir). Lands when `@mirror/store`'s
#                    git-backed implementation lifts its disk
#                    surface."
#
# And io.mirror 380 verbatim (family-root's forward-promise
# aggregate): "@io/fs, @io/git, @io/process: lift when the runtime
# layer's disk / git / subprocess surfaces consume them." @io/git
# lifted 2026-06-24 (commit 2801478 sibling); Mara-mintable per
# @io family-root discipline. @io/fs discharges the disk-side
# clause of the same forward-promise.
#
# The consumer that pulls @io/fs at this landing: Arc-2.3
# peer_persistence.rs (bootstrap/src/peer_persistence.rs) via
# @io/secrets.materialize's disk-write. Alex 2026-07-14 SSH-
# signing design intent ("project visibility/private stuff onto
# disk through the Peers key") IS a `@mirror/store` disk-surface
# consumer per the forward-promise language.
#
# === Substrate-already-had-the-word citation chain ===
#
# The word `@io/fs` is substrate-decl'd across three altitudes
# with the primitive already operationally present:
#
#   1. `shards/io.mirror` 189-191 + 380 verbatim — family-root
#      forward-promise naming the POSIX filesystem primitives at
#      species altitude.
#
#   2. `boot/std/io.mirror` (2026-06-01) — boot-floor grammar
#      already declares open / close / read / write per the
#      UNIX file-descriptor discipline (io.mirror 12-14 verbatim:
#      "read / write / open / close").
#
#   3. `bootstrap/src/*.rs` — Rust bootstrap has been calling
#      std::fs primitives since T1; the mirror-altitude species
#      names the typed contract those Rust calls have been
#      implicitly discharging.
#
# The ~62nd instance of [[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-
# word]] on landing — POSIX filesystem primitives have been
# operational since bootstrap era; this shard names the mirror-
# altitude species that consumers already assumed existed.
#
# === Composition graph ===
#
# Parents (substrate-decl):
#   @io                       — family-root; the boundary-with-non-
#                               mirror discipline; five-op prism +
#                               opacity + imperfect return shape.
#
# Consumers (compose over this species):
#   @io/secrets               — key-gated sealing; materialize
#                               action writes ciphertext to disk
#                               via write; open action reads
#                               ciphertext from disk via read.
#   @io/secrets/sops          — SOPS sub-species; sops_encrypt +
#                               sops_decrypt read/write .sops.yaml
#                               files via this species.
#   @io/git                   — future re-route: git object
#                               database read/write (currently
#                               shell-bridged; future gitoxide
#                               composition would pull this shard).
#   @mirror/store             — git-backed storage the forward-
#                               promise named as the pulling
#                               consumer.
#
# === Cross-wall-kintsugi candidacy partition ===
#
# Per @io family-root discipline (io.mirror 80-92; glass-wall
# insight): POSIX filesystem primitives are PERMANENT @io. Kernel-
# managed file descriptors, blocking reads, filesystem-metadata
# caching are structurally non-foldable into mirror altitude —
# they stay in @io forever. No cross-wall-kintsugi candidacy at
# this species; all sub-actions inherit permanent-@io status.
#
# === Related shards / specs ===
#
# - shards/io.mirror 189-191 + 380 (family-root forward-promise
#   this shard discharges).
# - boot/std/io.mirror (boot-floor read/write/open/close grammar;
#   retained transitional).
# - shards/io/git.mirror (sibling lift-tick precedent from
#   2026-06-24; same forward-promise language governs both).
# - shards/io/crypto.mirror (sibling lift-tick landed alongside
#   this tick per Seam §D9).
# - shards/io/secrets.mirror (primary consumer landed alongside
#   this tick).
# - docs/scouts/2026-07-15-mara-secrets-shape-proposal.md §5.5
#   step 7 (the landing sequence this shard discharges).
# - docs/audits/2026-07-15-seam-secrets-shape-proposal-phase-d-
#   cascade.md §D7 (Scenario B collapses to Scenario A via
#   @io/git precedent; @io/fs is Mara-mintable).
#
# === Substrate decisions ===
#
# [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
# [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]] (`prism @io/fs`),
# [[architecture-glass-wall-substrate-types]] (transparency inheritance
#   through @io),
# [[architecture-lift-as-load-bearing]] (forward-promise-altitude
#   → mirror-altitude species; same lift that gave @io/git),
# [[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word]] (~62nd instance;
#   POSIX filesystem has been operational since bootstrap era),
# [[feedback-substrate-pull]] (the species pulls @io/fs from
#   forward-promise to mirror altitude; no invention, only lift),
# [[feedback-no-bare-types]] (path, file_metadata, dir_entry are
#   typed carriers; never bare strings/scalars at the boundary),
# [[feedback-craft-not-deliver]] (all action bodies `\`-obligation-
#   blocked; consumers pull the realization; Arc-1 evaluator FLOOR
#   pending).
#
# Path-namespace property: this file at `shards/io/fs.mirror`
# declares `@io/fs` per `@epistemologic/pact/path_matches_
# namespace`.

prism @io/fs {
  focus  fs
  project fs
  split  fs
  shift  fs
  settle fs
}

# ============================================================
# §2 The carriers
# ============================================================

# === The path carrier ===
#
# Typed reference for a POSIX filesystem path. Per POSIX.1-2017
# §3.271 (Pathname): a null-terminated character string admitting
# an absolute (leading /) or relative (no leading /) form; path
# components separated by /; total length bounded by PATH_MAX
# (typically 4096 on Linux; 1024 on Darwin).
#
# Two-field record:
#
#   segments    — typed ref to the path's segments (per @io/uri
#                 the substrate's canonical path-decomposition
#                 discipline; the segment list is byte-visible).
#   is_absolute — bool discriminating absolute vs relative;
#                 byte-visible from the leading-/ check.
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on (segments, is_absolute).
# Two path values with the same segment sequence and same absolute-
# ness are the same path under content-addressing.
type path = {
  segments:    ref,
  is_absolute: bool,
}

# === The file_metadata carrier ===
#
# POSIX file metadata per stat(2). Typed record of the load-bearing
# fields; the raw stat_t struct is opaque per @io discipline; this
# carrier surfaces the fields substrate consumers read.
#
# Four-field record:
#
#   size         — file size in bytes (u64 per POSIX).
#   file_type    — typed ref discriminating file / directory /
#                  symlink / other (per S_IFMT + S_ISREG / S_ISDIR /
#                  S_ISLNK); byte-visible from the mode word.
#   permissions  — typed ref carrying the POSIX permission bits
#                  (rwx per user/group/other + sticky/setuid/setgid);
#                  9-bit or 12-bit representation.
#   mtime        — modification timestamp (typed ref to the
#                  seconds+nanoseconds pair per struct timespec).
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on (size, file_type,
# permissions, mtime).
type file_metadata = {
  size:        ref,
  file_type:   ref,
  permissions: ref,
  mtime:       ref,
}

# === The dir_entry carrier ===
#
# Per-entry record from readdir(3). Typed record naming a directory
# entry's substrate-visible fields.
#
# Two-field record:
#
#   name       — typed ref to the entry's filename (relative to
#                the enclosing directory; UTF-8 byte sequence per
#                the substrate's text convention; POSIX admits
#                arbitrary bytes but this carrier constrains to
#                UTF-8 for substrate legibility).
#   file_type  — typed ref discriminating file / directory /
#                symlink / other (parallels file_metadata.file_type
#                for early-typed dispatch without a stat call).
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on (name, file_type).
type dir_entry = {
  name:      ref,
  file_type: ref,
}

# ============================================================
# §3 The actions
# ============================================================

# === read action ===
#
# [Note: renamed from fs_read per 5dcad39 §2.5.g Q_D POSIX-inertia
# — bare verb matches boot/std/io.mirror precedent (open/read/
# write/close at boot floor) and store.mirror bare read/write.]
#
# Read the entire contents of a file at path. Per the POSIX
# open(O_RDONLY) + read + close sequence; substrate carries the
# whole-file byte sequence as ref.
#
# requires path_admissible(p): the path MUST be well-formed per
# POSIX pathname discipline.
#
# Returns imperfect<ref, ref, ref>:
# - success(bytes_ref) — read complete; bytes_ref resolves to
#   the file's content bytes.
# - partial(bytes_ref, transparency) — read complete with residual
#   (e.g., partial read on interrupt; short read; readable but
#   read-through-truncation).
# - failure(error, transparency) — read failed (ENOENT, EACCES,
#   EIO, file too large for whole-file read).
#
# Body discharges at the realisation boundary (std::fs::read on
# the Rust side; kernel read(2) at the syscall boundary).
read(p: path) -> imperfect { \ }

# === write action ===
#
# [Note: renamed from fs_write per 5dcad39 §2.5.h Q_D POSIX-inertia
# — same bare-verb precedent as `read`.]
#
# Write bytes to a file at path. Creates the file if absent;
# truncates + rewrites if present. Per POSIX open(O_WRONLY |
# O_CREAT | O_TRUNC) + write + close.
#
# requires path_admissible(p): the path MUST be well-formed.
#
# Returns imperfect<ref, ref, ref>:
# - success(bytes_written_ref) — write complete; ref resolves to
#   the count of bytes written (u64).
# - partial(bytes_written_ref, transparency) — partial write
#   (short write; disk-full recovery admitted).
# - failure(error, transparency) — write failed (EACCES, ENOSPC,
#   EROFS, path parent missing).
#
# THE LOAD-BEARING action for @io/secrets.materialize: the disk-
# write surface that Alex 2026-07-14's design intent ("project
# visibility/private stuff onto disk") composes over.
#
# Body discharges at the realisation boundary.
write(p: path, bytes: ref) -> imperfect { \ }

# === stat action ===
#
# [Note: renamed from fs_stat per 5dcad39 §2.5.i Q_D POSIX-inertia
# — POSIX stat(2) is the vendor primitive.]
#
# POSIX stat(2). Returns file_metadata for the path; follows
# symlinks (per stat, not lstat). For lstat semantics, a species
# refinement can land.
#
# requires path_admissible(p): the path MUST be well-formed.
#
# Returns imperfect<file_metadata, ref, ref>:
# - success(file_metadata) — stat complete; carrier populated.
# - failure(error, transparency) — stat failed (ENOENT, EACCES,
#   ELOOP).
#
# Body discharges at the realisation boundary (std::fs::metadata).
stat(p: path) -> imperfect { \ }

# === readdir action ===
#
# [Note: renamed from fs_list per 5dcad39 §2.5.j Q_D — Seam-
# preferred over `list`: matches POSIX readdir(3) vendor primitive
# AND boot/std/io.mirror precedent naming.]
#
# POSIX readdir(3). Returns the list of dir_entry values for
# entries in the directory at path (excluding . and ..).
#
# requires path_admissible(p): the path MUST be well-formed.
#
# Returns imperfect<ref, ref, ref>:
# - success(entries_ref) — readdir complete; entries_ref resolves
#   to the list of dir_entry values (byte-visible list; unordered
#   per POSIX).
# - partial(entries_ref, transparency) — readdir complete with
#   residual (e.g., some entries had unreadable metadata).
# - failure(error, transparency) — readdir failed (ENOENT,
#   ENOTDIR, EACCES).
#
# Body discharges at the realisation boundary (std::fs::read_dir).
readdir(p: path) -> imperfect { \ }

# === mkdir action ===
#
# [Note: renamed from fs_mkdir per 5dcad39 §2.5.k Q_D POSIX-inertia
# — POSIX mkdir(2) is the vendor primitive.]
#
# POSIX mkdir(2). Creates the directory at path. Idempotent-mode
# discrimination (mkdir vs mkdir -p) is species-refinement scope;
# this action creates ONE directory and fails if any parent is
# absent.
#
# requires path_admissible(p): the path MUST be well-formed.
#
# Returns imperfect<ref, ref, ref>:
# - success(created_ref) — mkdir complete.
# - failure(error, transparency) — mkdir failed (EEXIST, ENOENT,
#   EACCES, EROFS).
#
# Body discharges at the realisation boundary (std::fs::create_dir).
mkdir(p: path) -> imperfect { \ }

# ============================================================
# §4 The bilaterals (Rice-safe at whole-tick altitude)
# ============================================================

# === path_admissible predicate ===
#
# Bilateral verdict that a path is well-formed per POSIX pathname
# discipline:
#
#   1. Total length within PATH_MAX (byte-visible from segments
#      length).
#   2. No embedded NUL bytes in segments (POSIX pathname rules).
#   3. Each segment within NAME_MAX (typically 255).
#
# Rice-safe at whole-tick altitude per Mara-B §4.5.5: reads only
# byte-visible path structure; no filesystem I/O required.
#
# Splinter-pole: path is POSIX-well-formed; syscalls accept it.
# Narcissus-pole: path exceeds bounds or contains NUL; syscalls
# would reject.
path_admissible(p: path) -> verdict { \ }

# === path_exists predicate ===
#
# Bilateral verdict that a path resolves to an extant filesystem
# entry. Per POSIX access(F_OK); discharges at the @io boundary.
#
# Rice-safe at whole-tick altitude: reads only the filesystem's
# byte-visible directory entries; no program semantics.
#
# Splinter-pole: path exists; stat would succeed.
# Narcissus-pole: path does not exist OR path has an unreadable
# parent (indistinguishable at this bilateral; species refinements
# can distinguish).
path_exists(p: path) -> verdict { \ }

# === path_writable predicate ===
#
# [Note: renamed from `writable` per 5dcad39 shard-consistency —
# matches sibling `path_admissible` / `path_exists` shard-internal
# naming pattern; bilateral names its subject explicitly.]
#
# Bilateral verdict that a path is writable by the current
# process. Per POSIX access(W_OK).
#
# For non-extant paths: path_writable iff the parent directory
# exists AND is writable by the current process (write would
# succeed to create).
#
# Rice-safe at whole-tick altitude: reads only byte-visible
# permission bits + directory entries; bounded by the path's
# depth.
#
# Splinter-pole: write would succeed at this path.
# Narcissus-pole: permission denied, filesystem read-only, path
# parent missing.
path_writable(p: path) -> verdict { \ }

# === fs_well_formed composed bilateral ===
#
# THE LOAD-BEARING composed bilateral at @io/fs altitude. Composes:
#
#   1. path_admissible(p)
#   2. path_exists(p) OR path_writable(p) — read-side OR write-side
#      admissibility per the calling action's intent.
#
# Sibling to @io/oci.oci_well_formed, @io/git.git_well_formed,
# @io/algebra.io_algebra_well_formed, @io/crypto.crypto_well_formed
# — the composed-bilateral pattern at @io species altitude (per
# Seam tick 68 C4/C9 closure).
#
# Splinter-pole: path is well-formed AND admissible for the
# intended operation.
# Narcissus-pole: any sub-check fails; alignment-as-boundary-
# mathematics (#57) distinguishes which claim broke.
fs_well_formed(p: path) -> verdict
requires path_admissible(p)
{ \ }

out @io/fs
out path
out file_metadata
out dir_entry
out read
out write
out stat
out readdir
out mkdir
out path_admissible
out path_exists
out path_writable
out fs_well_formed
